# Advice for a Dollar — settings
#
# Copy this file to one called `.env` in the project folder and fill it in.
# The server reads it every time it starts. `.env` is ignored by Git, so
# nothing you put here is ever uploaded.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Admin password — the one setting needed to run the site.
#
# Run `npm run admin:hash`, type a password, and paste the two lines it prints
# below. The password itself is never stored anywhere: only this hash and salt
# (scrypt, built into Node). Lost the password? Run the command again.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH=
ADMIN_PASSWORD_SALT=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional. Remove the leading # to use one.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Port for the API server. The website expects 8787.
# PORT=8787

# Where the database lives. One file holds everything; back it up by copying it.
# DATABASE_PATH=data/advice.db

# Set on a real server. Turns on Secure cookies and strict-transport headers.
# NODE_ENV=production

# When the site sits behind a reverse proxy or a tunnel (nginx, Caddy,
# Cloudflare), every connection reaches the server from the proxy itself.
# This tells the server to take the real visitor address from the proxy.
# Without it the login and recovery limits treat everyone as one visitor,
# and eight wrong guesses by anyone lock the owner out for fifteen minutes.
# `loopback` is right for a proxy on the same machine. Leave it unset when
# running on your own computer.
# TRUST_PROXY=loopback

# Payments run on a built-in pretend provider, and nothing is charged. The
# Epoint and Payriff adapters refuse to run until their documentation arrives;
# their credentials, and the email (SMTP) settings, will be added here then.
